Microsoft Learn: Introduction to ARM Templates

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Introduction to ARM Templates
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to use Azure Resource Manager (ARM) templates to deploy and manage Azure infrastructure through Infrastructure as Code (IaC).

This module introduced the fundamentals of ARM templates, including template structure, resource declarations, parameters, and outputs. The training emphasized creating reusable and consistent Azure deployments across multiple environments.

Step 1: Understanding Infrastructure as Code (IaC)

The module introduced the concept of Infrastructure as Code, where infrastructure is defined through configuration files instead of manual deployment through the Azure portal.

Key benefits of IaC:
Consistent deployments
Version-controlled infrastructure
Repeatable environments
Easier collaboration between teams
Reduced configuration drift
Faster deployment automation

The scenario focused on deploying inventory systems for multiple partner companies, each with different Azure storage configurations and policies.

ARM templates help standardize deployments while still allowing customization for each environment.

Step 2: Introduction to ARM Templates

ARM templates are JSON-based files used to define Azure infrastructure declaratively.

Important ARM template components:
Component	Purpose
Parameters	Accept custom deployment values
Variables	Store reusable values
Resources	Define Azure resources
Outputs	Return deployment information
Key concepts learned:
ARM templates describe the desired infrastructure state
Azure Resource Manager handles deployment orchestration
Templates can deploy multiple resources together
Infrastructure definitions can be stored in source control systems like GitHub

This approach improves deployment reliability and team collaboration.

Step 3: Creating ARM Templates in Visual Studio Code

The module demonstrated creating ARM templates using Visual Studio Code.

Skills practiced:
Creating JSON ARM template files
Defining template schema versions
Structuring template sections correctly
Using IntelliSense and extensions for validation
Editing and testing deployment configurations
Example resources deployed:
Azure Storage Accounts
Resource groups
Configurable deployment settings

Visual Studio Code improves the authoring experience by helping validate ARM template syntax and structure.

Step 4: Using Parameters and Outputs

A major focus of the module was adding flexibility to deployments through parameters and outputs.

Parameters allow:
Different resource names
Different regions
Different SKU selections
Environment-specific configurations
Outputs provide:
Resource IDs
Storage account names
Endpoint URLs
Deployment information for later use
Key lessons learned:
Parameters make templates reusable across environments
Outputs simplify integration with automation workflows
Flexible templates reduce duplication and manual changes

This practice supports scalable enterprise deployments.

Step 5: Deploying ARM Templates

The module covered deploying ARM templates through command-line tools.

Deployment tools introduced:
Azure CLI
Azure PowerShell
Azure Portal deployment options
Important deployment concepts:
Resource group deployments
Validation before deployment
Incremental deployment behavior
Infrastructure version management

Using deployment automation improves consistency and reduces human error.

Step 6: ARM Templates vs Bicep

The module also introduced Bicep as a modern alternative to raw JSON ARM templates.

Key differences:
ARM Templates	Bicep
JSON syntax	Simpler syntax
Verbose structure	Cleaner and more readable
Native Azure support	Native Azure support
More complex authoring	Easier development experience

Microsoft recommends Bicep for new Infrastructure as Code projects because of its improved readability and developer experience.

